  "slug": "arabidopsis-mediated-f99c",
  "summary": "Recent Arabidopsis and rose studies resolve hormone-gene regulation to specific promoters, chromatin marks and splice events.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is regulation of hormone signalling at defined molecular targets, with abscisic acid (ABA) the most frequent node. In rose, RhWUS1 directly represses RhNCED3, reducing ABA biosynthesis, and is itself regulated by cytokinin and auxin factors that control axillary bud outgrowth. In Arabidopsis, SRPKII kinases modulate HAB1 alternative splicing through SR45 phosphorylation, shaping ABA sensitivity during seedling establishment. Other entries extend this framework to chromatin and stress: RSI1/FLD removes H3K4me2/3 at its own transcription start site to limit pathogen-induced expression, and an RHD6-directed network sustains root hair growth under combined low phosphate and salt stress. What is new is resolution to specific promoters, epigenetic marks and splice events.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
